{"dir":"out","ms":9175,"line":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":4,"method":"session/prompt","params":{"sessionId":"0b61a073-55b9-4a87-b695-d618ec45a6e0","prompt":[{"type":"text","text":"The workspace is `{{CWD}}`, not the directory you start in: name its files by their full path there.\n\nThis task is read-only: your own file edits, shell and web tools are off. Read files, search the repository and run tests with `run_command`, read web pages with `fetch` and search the web with `web_search`.\n\nWhen you have fully completed this task, call the `task_complete` tool with status `done` and your handoff. If you cannot complete it, call `task_complete` with status `blocked` or `failed` and the reason instead. Call it once, as your last action.\n\nIts `summary` is this task's handoff to the next task: the planner and the tasks that depend on this one read it first, and nothing else you write reaches them unless they ask. Write it for that reader, not as a log: what changed, what is left, what to watch. It is at most 1500 characters, and a call over a limit is refused: the long form goes in `report`.\n\nPut anything the next reader must act on in `findings`, one item each, most important first, each with a severity, a one-line title, its detail and evidence to check it by: a file (and line), a commit, a task (and one of its findings, as \"F3\") or a URL. Ordewell numbers them F1, F2, … so a later task can cite one. List what you produced in `artifacts`.\n\nThe long story, if there is one, goes in `report`: it is kept with this attempt and read only on demand. A review or other read-only task especially: its findings are its result, so state each one, and do not write the report to a file.\n\nFind the secret word that README.md in the workspace names, then complete the task through Ordewell with status \"done\" and the secret word as the summary."}]}}}
